<body>
    <h1>AI-267: Facial Recognition: Integrating Temporal Tracking for Enhan=
ced Performance</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <h1 id=3D"AI-267:FacialRecognition:IntegratingTemporalTrackingforEn=
hancedPerformance-CurrentImplementation">Current Implementation</h1>
<p>The initial approach we implemented relies on a basic "sample every fram=
e" strategy for face tracking:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Go back 1 second before the sample location.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Sample every frame to check for the face.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Store the first frame where the face is found as the Inpoint.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Continue sampling every frame.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Save the previous frame as the outpoint frame when the face disappears.<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p>Use inPoint and outPoint to generate accurate inpoint and outpoint and d=
uration information.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>This approach essentially re-runs face detection and embedding extractio=
n on <em>every single frame</em> (or at least within a 1-second window) of =
the video.</p>
<p>This approach has several critical flaws, making it unsuitable for the d=
emands of our project, <em>especially</em> given the limitations of our cur=
rent workstation:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Computational Overload:</strong> The core problem is sheer compu=
tational cost. Face detection and embedding extraction are computationally =
intensive tasks, even with a powerful GPU like the L40S. Re-running these o=
perations on every frame of high-resolution video creates a massive process=
ing bottleneck. The GPU simply cannot keep up with the workload, which coul=
d lead to extremely slow processing times and potentially causing system cr=
ashes or instability.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Redundancy and Inefficiency:</strong> Consecutive video frames o=
ften contain very similar information. Re-analyzing each frame from scratch=
 wastes valuable GPU cycles on redundant computations. We're essentially re=
-discovering the same information over and over.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Lack of Temporal Coherence:</strong> The algorithm ignores the f=
act that faces in a video typically move smoothly from frame to frame. It d=
oesn't leverage the temporal relationships between frames, which could sign=
ificantly reduce the computational burden.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Poor Handling of Occlusions:</strong> Sports content, particular=
ly baseball, is rife with occlusions. A player's face might be hidden by a =
glove, a helmet, or another player. The "sample every frame" approach fails=
 as soon as an occlusion occurs, leading to premature track termination and=
 inaccurate results.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Sensitivity to Appearance Changes:</strong> The appearance of a =
face can change due to lighting, pose, and expression. The initial embeddin=
g might become less accurate over time, causing the algorithm to lose track=
.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Arbitrary Sampling Window:</strong> The 1-second sampling window=
 is an arbitrary and inflexible parameter. It's not adaptive to the actual =
video content or the movement of the faces.</p></li>
</ul>
<h1 id=3D"AI-267:FacialRecognition:IntegratingTemporalTrackingforEnhancedPe=
rformance-Design">Design</h1>
<p>I propose we move to a more intelligent and efficient system built aroun=
d object tracking, multi-model face recognition, and careful resource manag=
ement.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Accurate:</strong> Lower false positives, higher confidence in i=
dentity assignments.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Efficient:</strong> Maximized GPU throughput, reduced processing=
 time.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Scalable:</strong> Handles multiple faces and long video sequenc=
es.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Robust:</strong> Tolerant of occlusions, lighting variations, an=
d pose changes.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Maintainable:</strong> Modular design for easier debugging and m=
odification.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Here's a breakdown of the key components:</p>
<p>NOTE: This algorithm is portable, extending to the Object Detection fram=
ework!</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-267:FacialRecognition:IntegratingTemporalTrackingforEnhancedPe=
rformance-Multi-ObjectTracking:"><strong>Multi-Object Tracking:</strong></h=
2>
<p>We're integrating the DeepSORT (Deep Simple Online and Realtime Tracking=
) algorithm. DeepSORT isn't just a tracker; it's a complete tracking-by-det=
ection system. It handles:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Object Detection:</strong> DeepSORT <em>incorporates</em> an obj=
ect detector. While we can experiment with different detectors, we'll likel=
y start with a RetinaFace model running within DeepSORT.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>State Estimation:</strong> DeepSORT uses a Kalman filter to pred=
ict the future location of each tracked object based on its past trajectory=
. This is crucial for handling brief occlusions and noisy detections.</p></=
li>
<li>
<p><strong>Data Association:</strong> DeepSORT uses the Hungarian algorithm=
 to associate new detections with existing tracks based on both location an=
d appearance.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Appearance Modeling:</strong> The "deep" part of DeepSORT comes =
from its use of a deep learning-based appearance descriptor (often a CNN) t=
o capture the visual characteristics of each tracked object. This allows De=
epSORT to re-identify objects even after significant changes in pose or lig=
hting.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>DeepSORT provides the robust foundation we need to track multiple player=
s throughout the game, even with frequent occlusions and complex camera mov=
ements. It offloads the majority of the frame-by-frame analysis from our fa=
ce recognition models, freeing up the L40S for more strategic tasks.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-267:FacialRecognition:IntegratingTemporalTrackingforEnhancedPe=
rformance-PeriodicVerification:"><strong>Periodic Verification:</strong></h=
2>
<p>DeepSORT provides <em>track IDs</em> and bounding boxes. However, these =
are just for tracking <em>objects</em>. We need to identify <em>who</em> th=
ose objects are. The following is a rough outline of the algorithm:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Every <em>N</em> frames (we'll experiment with values like 10, 15, or 20=
, balancing accuracy and performance), we'll extract the face region from t=
he bounding box provided by DeepSORT.</p></li>
<li>
<p>We'll extract embeddings using the configured face recognition models (e=
.g. FaceNet512 and ArcFace).</p></li>
<li>
<p>We'll compare these embeddings to our database of known players.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">      for=
 track in deepsort.tracker.tracks:
    if not track.is_confirmed() or track.time_since_update &gt; 1:
        continue  # Skip unconfirmed or recently lost tracks

    track_id =3D track.track_id
    bbox =3D track.to_tlwh()  # Get bounding box (x, y, width, height)

    if frame_count % REID_FREQUENCY =3D=3D 0:
        face_region =3D extract_face_from_frame(frame, bbox)
        if face_region is not None: #Handle for if a face cannot be extract=
ed.
            facenet_embedding =3D extract_embedding(face_region, model=3D"F=
aceNet512")
            arcface_embedding =3D extract_embedding(face_region, model=3D"A=
rcFace")

            # Compare embeddings to our database
            best_match, confidence =3D find_best_match(facenet_embedding, a=
rcface_embedding, database)

            if confidence &gt; IDENTITY_THRESHOLD: # We can set this for ea=
ch type of model for better accuracy.
                track.identity =3D best_match
                cache_identity(track_id, best_match) #We can set a timer fo=
r each individual for better accuracy.
    </pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-267:FacialRecognition:IntegratingTemporalTrackingforEnhancedPe=
rformance-ConfidenceScoring"><strong>Confidence Scoring</strong></h2>
<p>Cosine similarity scores are just that =E2=80=93 <em>similarity</em> sco=
res. They don't tell us anything about the <em>reliability</em> of the mode=
l or the certainty of the match. We need a confidence metric. So we will pe=
rform the following steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Establish baseline cosine similarity scores for each model (FaceNet512, =
ArcFace) on our dataset. This will help us understand the typical range of =
scores for correct matches.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Combine the cosine similarity scores from the different models using a w=
eighted average. Models with higher baseline reliability will receive highe=
r weights.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Implement penalties for scores that are significantly below the baseline=
 or for cases where the face is partially occluded.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Consider the context of the track. If a player has been consistently ide=
ntified as "Shohei Ohtani" for the past 3 minutes, we should be more confid=
ent in subsequent matches.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">    def f=
ind_best_match(facenet_embedding, arcface_embedding, database):
    best_match =3D None
    best_confidence =3D 0.0
    for identity, embeddings in database.items():
        facenet_similarity =3D cosine_similarity(facenet_embedding, embeddi=
ngs["facenet"]) #Assume the database structure is the same as the extractio=
n
        arcface_similarity =3D cosine_similarity(arcface_embedding, embeddi=
ngs["arcface"])

        # Weighted average of the similarities
        confidence =3D (FACENET_WEIGHT * facenet_similarity + ARCFACE_WEIGH=
T * arcface_similarity)

        if confidence &gt; best_confidence:
            best_confidence =3D confidence
            best_match =3D identity

    return best_match, best_confidence
    </pre>
</div>
</div></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-267:FacialRecognition:IntegratingTemporalTrackingforEnhancedPe=
rformance-IdentityManagement"><strong>Identity Management</strong></h2>
<p>Once we've confidently identified a player, we'll cache this association=
 in a lookup table (keyed by the DeepSORT track ID). For subsequent frames,=
 we'll <em>prioritize</em> the identity matching against the cached identit=
y. If the cached identity matches with high confidence, we can skip the ful=
l database search. We will only perform this when REID_FREQUENCY is met.</p=
>
<p>The vast majority of tracks will represent the same player for extended =
periods. Caching the identity significantly reduces the computational load =
on the GPU</p>
<p>Because this is DeepSORT, we can assume that if the identity cache exist=
s, the identity stays relatively the same after we have run and generated a=
 cache key.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">def cache=
_identity(track_id, identity):
    identity_cache[track_id] =3D {
        "identity": identity,
        "timestamp": current_time()
    }

def check_cache(track_id):
    #If the track_id exists in identity_cache and timer is running for the =
correct timestamp then run this code.
    if track_id in identity_cache:
        identity_information=3D identity_cache[track_id]
        #Run processes here.
        del identity_cache[track_id]
        return identity_information
    </pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p>
    </div>
